cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use PALS's Law in your work, please cite it as below."
title: "PALS's Law: A Formal Specification of LLM Output Unreliability as an Architectural Invariant"
type: report
authors:
  - family-names: "de Luna e Silva"
    given-names: "Pedro Anisio"
    alias: "PALS"
version: "1.5.4"
date-released: "2026-04-03"
license: "CC-BY-4.0"
keywords:
  - "LLM"
  - "large language models"
  - "hallucination"
  - "verification"
  - "software engineering"
  - "architectural invariant"
  - "error taxonomy"
abstract: >-
  PALS's Law is an engineering principle asserting that LLM output error is
  not an exceptional condition but a statistical invariant of the model class,
  and that any system failing to treat it as such contains an architectural
  defect. The specification provides a formal operative claim, a 9-class error
  taxonomy, a pipeline compounding corollary, 5 architectural corollaries,
  and copy-paste practitioner artifacts.
